WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
A practical ISO/IEC 42001 implementation begins with context, not with control selection.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:15.080
Consider a regional insurer that uses AI for claims triage, customer email routing, fraud alerts, and employee productivity tools.

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:27.980
If the organization starts by copying controls into a policy library, it may miss the real issue: which AI activities matter to its objectives, obligations, stakeholders, and risk appetite.

4
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.680
Context prevents the AIMS from becoming either too narrow to matter or too broad to operate.

5
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:47.800
Internal context includes strategy, AI maturity, technical architecture, data quality, workforce competence, existing management systems, decision culture, business dependencies, and monitoring capability.

6
00:00:47.900 --> 00:01:02.060
External context includes laws, customer expectations, supplier dependencies, market pressure, security threats, public trust, industry norms, contractual obligations, and social or environmental concerns.

7
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.920
These issues shape what the AIMS must be capable of controlling.

8
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:14.980
A small organization may use lightweight procedures, while a regulated enterprise may need more formal approval, evidence, and escalation.

9
00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:20.860
Interested parties are people or groups that may affect the AIMS or be affected by AI use.

10
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:36.160
They can include customers, users, employees, applicants, patients, students, suppliers, regulators, business partners, communities, investors, internal governance bodies, and civil-society groups.

11
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.260
The organization should determine which needs and expectations are relevant.

12
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:47.560
It should not treat every request as binding, but it should not ignore foreseeable effects simply because they sit outside the IT department.

13
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.800
Scope translates context into boundaries.

14
00:01:50.900 --> 00:02:04.500
A useful AIMS scope identifies business functions, locations, products or services, AI activities, systems, supporting processes, dependencies, interfaces with other management systems, and justified exclusions.

15
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:11.160
A scope designed mainly to exclude the highest-risk AI use is a weak governance signal.

16
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.100
Scope should make accountability clearer, not make risk disappear.

17
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:24.000
The scope also helps auditors, managers, and system owners understand which evidence belongs to the AIMS and which interfaces need coordination.

18
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.880
An AI-system inventory then gives the organization visibility.

19
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:49.100
A proportionate inventory may capture the system name, owner, provider, intended purpose, users, affected parties, lifecycle state, deployment environment, data sources, service dependencies, decision significance, risk classification, impact-assessment status, monitoring status, last review, and retirement plan.

20
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.600
It should include externally supplied AI and shadow AI where material.

21
00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:00.980
The inventory does not need to turn every ordinary rule or spreadsheet into an AI record, but it should reveal material AI dependencies.

22
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:08.360
The synthesis is direct: an organization cannot govern AI systems it has not identified and brought into an accountable scope.

23
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:20.780
The first implementation milestone is therefore visibility, not perfection: know enough about the AI estate to assign ownership, prioritize assessment, and bring unmanaged use into review.
